var e={updated:`2026-11-07`,brands:[{slug:`michelin`,name:`Michelin`,title:`Michelin Tyres in Singapore: History, Models and How to Choose`,description:`Michelin history, ten current models and how to choose for school runs, commuting, SUVs and electric cars, written by an independent Singapore tyre retailer.`,lead:`Michelin is one of the most common names on a Singapore tyre sidewall, and one of the hardest to compare, because there are many models, some of them sold in only a few sizes, and almost every claim you read comes from Michelin itself. This section of our site is our attempt to do three things in one place: tell the story of the company, explain the models you are most likely to be offered, and show you how to choose for the way you actually drive.`,blocks:[{type:`h2`,text:`Start with the question you have`,id:`start-with-the-question-you-have`},{type:`table`,head:[`You want to know`,`Read`],rows:[[`Where Michelin came from and what it invented`,`[The history of Michelin](/tyre-brands/michelin/history/)`],[`Which Michelin suits school runs, the office commute and trips to KL`,`[Which Michelin for how you drive](/tyre-brands/michelin/which-michelin-for-your-driving/)`],[`Whether to buy the Primacy 5`,`[Michelin Primacy 5 review](/tyre-brands/michelin/primacy-5/)`],[`What the Pilot Sport 5 is and who it suits`,`[Michelin Pilot Sport 5](/tyre-brands/michelin/pilot-sport-5/)`],[`The older Pilot Sport 4 and the track-minded 4 S`,`[Pilot Sport 4 and 4 S](/tyre-brands/michelin/pilot-sport-4-and-4s/)`],[`What to put on an SUV`,`[Michelin tyres for SUVs](/tyre-brands/michelin/suv-tyres/)`],[`What to put on an electric car`,`[Michelin tyres for electric cars](/tyre-brands/michelin/ev-tyres/)`]]},{type:`h2`,text:`The ten models we cover, in one line each`,id:`the-ten-models-we-cover-in-one-line-each`},{type:`table`,head:[`Model`,`What Michelin says it is`,`Where to read more`],rows:[[`Primacy 5`,`Everyday comfort tyre with long life, also suited to EVs`,`[Primacy 5 review](/tyre-brands/michelin/primacy-5/)`],[`Energy XM2+`,`Long-life summer tyre for city and weekend driving`,`[In the Primacy 5 review](/tyre-brands/michelin/primacy-5/#energy-xm2-plus)`],[`Pilot Sport 5`,`Fifth-generation sport summer tyre for the road`,`[Pilot Sport 5](/tyre-brands/michelin/pilot-sport-5/)`],[`Pilot Sport 4`,`High-performance summer road tyre, the PS5's predecessor`,`[Pilot Sport 4 and 4 S](/tyre-brands/michelin/pilot-sport-4-and-4s/)`],[`Pilot Sport 4 S`,`Maximum-performance road and occasional track tyre`,`[Pilot Sport 4 and 4 S](/tyre-brands/michelin/pilot-sport-4-and-4s/)`],[`Pilot Sport 4 SUV`,`Premium, comfortable high-performance SUV tyre`,`[SUV tyres](/tyre-brands/michelin/suv-tyres/#pilot-sport-4-suv)`],[`Primacy SUV+`,`Summer SUV tyre focused on wet braking`,`[SUV tyres](/tyre-brands/michelin/suv-tyres/#primacy-suv-plus)`],[`Latitude Sport 3`,`High-performance SUV summer tyre`,`[SUV tyres](/tyre-brands/michelin/suv-tyres/#latitude-sport-3)`],[`e.Primacy`,`For electric, hybrid and low-consumption cars`,`[EV tyres](/tyre-brands/michelin/ev-tyres/#e-primacy)`],[`Pilot Sport EV`,`High-performance summer tyre for electric cars`,`[EV tyres](/tyre-brands/michelin/ev-tyres/#pilot-sport-ev)`]]},{type:`h2`,text:`How we write about Michelin`,id:`how-we-write-about-michelin`},{type:`p`,text:`We are an independent tyre retailer in Singapore. We are not an authorised Michelin dealer, and Michelin has not reviewed or approved these pages. Because we sell tyres, you should know the rules we set ourselves:`},{type:`ol`,items:[`**We separate Michelin's claims from other people's tests.** Anything taken from Michelin's own pages is marked "according to Michelin", and tests Michelin ordered are labelled as such.`,`**We report results that do not flatter the tyre.** If a model came last in a test, or has a weak point such as aquaplaning, we say so.`,`**We do not invent.** If we could not find a launch date or an independent test, the page says that instead of guessing.`,`**We keep prices off these pages.** Prices change; message us for options in your size.`]},{type:`p`,text:`If you spot a mistake, tell us on WhatsApp and we will check it and correct the page.`}],pages:[{slug:`history`,kind:`history`,title:`Michelin Tyres: History, Innovations and Tyre Families — A Singapore Driver's Guide`,short:`From a rubber workshop in 1889 to the radial tyre, the Guide and today`,description:`The history of Michelin from the 1889 rubber works in Clermont-Ferrand to the radial tyre, the Michelin Guide, motorsport and its tyre families, written for Singapore drivers.`,lead:`If you drive in Singapore, there is a good chance the tyres on your car, or on the car next to you at a red light, carry the Michelin name. This guide tells the story behind it: where the company came from, what it actually invented, why it keeps turning up in motorsport and in restaurant guides, and what the main tyre families mean when you are choosing a set for your own car.`,blocks:[{type:`h2`,text:`Michelin at a glance`,id:`michelin-at-a-glance`},{type:`table`,head:[``,``],rows:[[`Company name at founding`,`Michelin & Cie`],[`Year the Michelin name began`,`1889`],[`Home town`,`Clermont-Ferrand, in the Auvergne region of France`],[`Best-known firsts`,`Detachable bicycle tyre (1891), first car on air-filled tyres (1895), the radial tyre (patent filed 4 June 1946)`],[`Famous mascot`,`Bibendum, the Michelin Man (1898)`],[`Beyond tyres`,`The Michelin Guide, road maps, road signs, rail tyres`],[`Motorsport`,`Wins at Le Mans, the World Rally Championship, Formula 1 and MotoGP`]]},{type:`h2`,text:`1. Before Michelin: rubber comes to Clermont-Ferrand`,id:`1-before-michelin-rubber-comes-to-clermont-ferrand`},{type:`p`,text:`The story begins with a bouncing ball. In the early 1800s a Scottish woman, Elisabeth Pugh-Barker, married an Auvergne businessman named Édouard Daubrée. Her uncle was the chemist Charles Macintosh, who had worked out that rubber could be dissolved in benzine. Remembering the rubber balls he had made for her as a girl, she began making her own in her husband's workshop. That small habit is how rubber arrived in Clermont-Ferrand.`},{type:`p`,text:`Daubrée later teamed up with his cousin Aristide Barbier to build farm machinery. Their company, Barbier-Daubrée, won a name at international trade fairs partly because its machines used so many rubber parts: hoses, valves, joints and fittings.`},{type:`p`,text:`When both founders died in the 1860s, the business was in trouble. Aristide Barbier's daughter Adèle, who had married Jules Michelin, believed rubber still had a future. She asked her sons André and Édouard to step in, and Édouard took charge and renamed the firm Michelin & Cie. The brothers' first own product, in 1889, was a rubber brake pad for horse-drawn cabs and carriages. They called it "The Silent". It was the first rubber object Michelin made and sold, and it already carried the first Michelin logo.`},{type:`h2`,text:`2. 1891: the modern tyre is born`,id:`2-1891-the-modern-tyre-is-born`},{type:`p`,text:`One day a cyclist with a flat tyre wheeled his bicycle into the factory yard. In those days bicycle tyres were glued onto the rim. The method held well, but repairing a puncture meant hours of scraping off glue and waiting for new glue to dry.`},{type:`p`,text:`Édouard Michelin took the problem personally and designed a detachable tyre. With it, mending a puncture took about fifteen minutes instead of hours. The idea proved itself quickly: the cyclist Charles Terront rode Michelin detachable tyres to win the Paris–Brest–Paris race, finishing more than eight hours ahead of the runner-up. For a young company, that was the best advertisement imaginable.`},{type:`h2`,text:`3. 1895: the first car to run on air`,id:`3-1895-the-first-car-to-run-on-air`},{type:`p`,text:`André and Édouard expected the motor car to grow fast, so they applied the same thinking to it. They fitted air-filled tyres to a car called L'Éclair and entered it in the Paris–Bordeaux–Paris race. L'Éclair did not win. But it became the first car in the world to run on air-filled tyres, and the brothers used the stunt to show drivers what pneumatic tyres promised: reliability, durability, safety and comfort.`},{type:`h2`,text:`4. 1898: Bibendum, the Michelin Man`,id:`4-1898-bibendum-the-michelin-man`},{type:`p`,text:`According to Michelin, the Michelin Man first took shape in a stack of tyres. His outline came from the imagination of André and Édouard and was drawn by the artist Marius Rossillon, known as O'Galop. The same year he appeared in a composition titled "Nunc est Bibendum" (Latin for "now is the time to drink") and made his first public appearance at the Michelin stand at the first Paris Motor Show.`},{type:`p`,text:`He has been working ever since. In 2000 the *Financial Times* named him "Best Logo of the Century", and in 2018, on his 120th anniversary, he received a Millennium Icon Award at Advertising Week in the United States.`},{type:`h2`,text:`5. 1899: the first car past 100 km/h`,id:`5-1899-the-first-car-past-100-km-h`},{type:`p`,text:`In 1899 the Belgian driver Camille Jenatzy wanted a speed record. Michelin equipped his electric car, La Jamais Contente ("Never Satisfied"), with special tyres. They were smaller in diameter and wider across the tread, which made the car steadier, and they already looked like the tyres we know today. With them, Jenatzy's car was the first in the world to pass 100 km/h.`},{type:`h2`,text:`6. 1900 to 1926: the guide, the map and the signpost`,id:`6-1900-to-1926-the-guide-the-map-and-the-signpost`},{type:`p`,text:`Early motoring was an adventure. Garages were rare, roads were poorly marked and a puncture could end a day. André Michelin saw that selling tyres meant helping people travel.`},{type:`ul`,items:[`**1900, the first Michelin Guide.** A small red booklet with tyre-repair advice and practical addresses: garages, fuel stops, tyre shops, hotels.`,`**1910, the road map.** A 1:200,000 map to go with the Guide, so drivers could find their way.`,`**1912, road numbering.** André campaigned for roads to be numbered. After the First World War he went further and had Michelin produce distance markers and road signs, made from enamelled lava plates on reinforced-concrete bases. Michelin built several thousand of them.`,`**1926, the star.** The Guide that introduced the "fine dining star" came out in 1926 and grew into a reference for people who care about food.`]},{type:`p`,text:`So when you see a "Michelin-starred" restaurant, the same company that made your tyres, and not a food publisher, is behind the name. It began as a tool for drivers.`},{type:`p`,text:`The Guide reached Singapore much later. The first Singapore edition was announced in late 2015 and revealed at a gala dinner at Resorts World Sentosa on 21 July 2016. Michelin Guide director Michael Ellis was reported as saying Singapore was the next Asian city for the Guide after Japan, Hong Kong and Macau, and the first in Southeast Asia (press reports; we have asked Michelin to confirm the details).`},{type:`h2`,text:`7. The First World War: tyres, tents and aeroplanes`,id:`7-the-first-world-war-tyres-tents-and-aeroplanes`},{type:`p`,text:`When 3,500 Michelin workers were called to the army, the company concentrated on tyres, wheels and essential rubber goods such as sleeping bags, tents and mugs. The brothers believed the war would be decided in the air, so part of the factory went over to building aircraft: from 1915 to 1918 the factory turned out close to 2,000 Bréguet aircraft. So that they could fly out in any conditions, the company laid what it describes as the world's first concrete runway, at Aulnat near Clermont-Ferrand (a 1916 photo caption on Michelin's heritage page dates the track).`},{type:`h2`,text:`8. 1931: the Micheline, a train on tyres`,id:`8-1931-the-micheline-a-train-on-tyres`},{type:`p`,text:`André Michelin, kept awake on a noisy sleeper train, wondered whether train wheels could run on tyres. The company designed a special tyre with a guide ring that could roll along the narrow rail surface and carry real weight. The result was the Micheline, a railcar on tyres. On 10 September 1931 prototype number 5 made its first trip between Paris and Deauville.`},{type:`h2`,text:`9. Citroën and the 2CV`,id:`9-citro-n-and-the-2cv`},{type:`p`,text:`Michelin gained control of the French carmaker Citroën, with Pierre Michelin and Pierre Boulanger running it. The pair began the TPV programme, an abbreviation of "Toute Petite Voiture" (very small car). The idea came from one of the first big consumer surveys in motoring, the National Popular Automobile Survey that the Michelin brothers ran in 1922: a car that would be simple and versatile, undemanding to drive and, first of all, cheap to own. It became the Citroën 2CV. Michelin's heritage material also mentions a recent project to bring the 2CV back for the age of electrification, to which Michelin says it contributes its expertise.`},{type:`h2`,text:`10. 1937: rubber meets steel`,id:`10-1937-rubber-meets-steel`},{type:`p`,text:`From around 1930 cars grew heavier and faster, and Michelin engineers rebuilt the tyre's structure. They added metal wires and spent about six years finding a way to make rubber and steel bond permanently. In 1937 the first steel-cased tyre went on sale. It was a quiet but important step: it made the next breakthrough possible.`},{type:`h2`,text:`11. 1946: the radial tyre`,id:`11-1946-the-radial-tyre`},{type:`p`,text:`The breakthrough came with a prototype nicknamed the "fly cage", because the cables in its casing were spaced so widely apart. Its design was the origin of the radial tyre. Michelin filed the patent on 4 June 1946, and the tyre went on sale as the **MICHELIN X**.`},{type:`p`,text:`The X tyre lasted two to four times longer than a conventional tyre, was safer, and used less fuel. Radial technology then spread to every kind of vehicle: heavy trucks (1952), civil-engineering vehicles (1959), farm tractors (1979), aircraft (1981) and motorcycles (1987).`},{type:`p`,text:`This is the main reason Michelin features in any history of tyres. Almost every car tyre sold today is a radial.`},{type:`p`,text:`François Michelin, grandson of the founder, became co-manager of the company alongside Robert Puiseux and pushed radial tyres into every car market. Under that strategy Michelin rose from tenth place to become the world's largest tyre maker.`},{type:`h3`,text:`Research and testing`,id:`research-and-testing`},{type:`p`,text:`To support the radial roll-out, Michelin put most of its money into developing the X tyre. It opened a research and test centre at Ladoux, near Clermont-Ferrand, in 1965, with laboratories, workshops and test tracks. Further centres came in Spain (Almería, 1973), the United States (Laurens, 1977) and Japan (Ota, 1991). Michelin also built unusual test vehicles. One of them, the *Milles Pates* ("Thousand Legs", also called the PLR for "Poids Lourd Rapide", or fast heavy truck), was built in 1972 to test truck tyres.`},{type:`h2`,text:`12. Motorsport: racing as a laboratory`,id:`12-motorsport-racing-as-a-laboratory`},{type:`p`,text:`Michelin stepped away from racing in 1912, then came back after the X tyre won the 24 Hours of Le Mans unexpectedly in 1951. A string of titles followed:`},{type:`ul`,items:[`**1973:** first World Rally Championship, with Alpine-Renault. The same year Michelin set up a dedicated motorsport department.`,`**1979:** Formula 1 World Championship.`,`**1984:** MotoGP World Championship.`,`**2018:** 400th MotoGP victory.`]},{type:`p`,text:`Michelin describes racing as a testing ground for new ideas, and it is one reason the Pilot Sport family you will read about below carries a sporting name.`},{type:`h2`,text:`13. Becoming a global company`,id:`13-becoming-a-global-company`},{type:`p`,text:`Success with the radial tyre pushed Michelin to expand. Starting in 1975 it opened four US plants, three in South Carolina (Greenville, Anderson, Spartanburg) and one in Alabama (Dothan), and the X tyre did well on other continents too. During the 1980s and 1990s the company reached emerging markets in Asia and Eastern Europe, with new plants in Asia (Japan, Thailand, Korea and China) and in Eastern Europe (Slovakia, Poland, the Czech Republic, Hungary and Russia).`},{type:`p`,text:`Growth also came by acquisition:`},{type:`table`,head:[`Year`,`Acquisition`],rows:[[`1981`,`Kléber`],[`1990`,`Uniroyal-Goodrich (opened up North America)`],[`1995`,`Stomil Olsztyn, Poland`],[`1996`,`Taurus, Hungary`]]},{type:`p`,text:`If you shop for tyres in Singapore, you will also see tyres made in several of these countries carrying the Michelin name.`},{type:`h2`,text:`14. The "green tyre" and fuel economy`,id:`14-the-green-tyre-and-fuel-economy`},{type:`p`,text:`In the early 1990s, when the environment was hardly a selling point, Michelin introduced the "green tyre". By adding silica to the rubber mix, it reduced rolling resistance, which lowers fuel use and CO2. The idea was shown at the Paris Motor Show in 1992 as the MXN (the N stands for *Nature*), and in 1994 launched the first range, called **MICHELIN Energy**.`},{type:`p`,text:`Rolling resistance still matters today, especially for electric cars, where it affects range.`},{type:`h2`,text:`15. Looking forward`,id:`15-looking-forward`},{type:`ul`,items:[`**Challenge Bibendum (1998–2014).** A series of 12 events around the world on clean and sustainable vehicles. In 2017 it became **Movin'On**, a world summit on sustainable mobility.`,`**UPTIS (2019).** An airless tyre concept designed so that punctures, and the need for a spare wheel, disappear.`,`**VISION concept.** A tyre imagined as biosourced, connected and with a renewable tread, covered by 19 patents and used as a guide for Michelin's research teams.`,`**Michelin in Motion (2021).** Michelin's strategy to 2030, combining tyre growth with connected mobility and high-technology materials. In 2023 it bought Flex Composite Group.`]},{type:`h2`,text:`15b. Beyond tyres: maps, travel services and lifestyle`,id:`15b-beyond-tyres-maps-travel-services-and-lifestyle`},{type:`p`,text:`Michelin has never been only a tyre company. Alongside the Guide and maps it built travel-assistance products and services for drivers. In 2000, the year the Michelin Man was named best logo of the century, the group created Michelin Lifestyle Limited, a subsidiary that designs and markets tie-in products for mobility, sport and leisure under licence with partner manufacturers. In the 2010s Michelin also bought the Spanish online restaurant-booking platform Restaurantes.com, which has since been renamed TheFork and operates in more than 20 European countries, according to Michelin's heritage page.`},{type:`h2`,text:`15c. Who has led Michelin`,id:`15c-who-has-led-michelin`},{type:`table`,head:[`Period`,`Leader(s)`,`Note`],rows:[[`1889 onwards`,`Édouard and André Michelin`,`Brothers who built Michelin & Cie`],[`Mid-1950s onwards`,`François Michelin (with Robert Puiseux)`,`Grandson of the founder; drove the worldwide switch to radial tyres`],[`2002`,`Édouard Michelin`,`Took over from his father at the May 2002 shareholders' meeting; died in an accident in 2006`],[`2005 to 2012`,`Michel Rollier`,`Sole managing partner after 2006`],[`2012 to 2019`,`Jean-Dominique Senard`,`Chairman of the group until May 2019`],[`2019 onwards`,`Florent Menegaux`,`CEO since the May 2019 shareholders' meeting`]]},{type:`h2`,text:`16. Three tyre ideas explained, using Michelin's own guidance`,id:`16-three-tyre-ideas-explained-using-michelin-s-own-guidance`},{type:`p`,text:`These explanations are based on Michelin Singapore's tyre advice pages. They are useful whatever brand you buy.`},{type:`h3`,text:`Rolling resistance: why tyres affect fuel use and EV range`,id:`rolling-resistance-why-tyres-affect-fuel-use-and-ev-range`},{type:`p`,text:`Rolling resistance is the energy a tyre loses as it rolls: the tyre deforms, it turns through air, and it rubs on the road. The higher it is, the more energy the car needs to keep moving. Material, structure, tread pattern, width, diameter and tyre pressure all change it.`},{type:`p`,text:`Michelin points to one number drivers can control: tyre pressure. It cites French road studies in which more than half of cars ran at least 0.3 bar below the correct pressure. According to Michelin, 0.3 bar of under-inflation raises rolling resistance by about 6%, and 1 bar by about 30%. (Check your own pressure against the placard on your car, [see our guide to tyre pressure in Singapore](/tyre-guides/car-tyre-pressure-singapore/).)`},{type:`p`,text:`You cannot remove rolling resistance entirely. The friction between tyre and road is also what gives you grip. The aim of a low-rolling-resistance design, such as the silica-based "green tyre" idea described earlier, is to lose less energy as heat without giving up grip.`},{type:`h3`,text:`Tread depth and wear indicators`,id:`tread-depth-and-wear-indicators`},{type:`p`,text:`Michelin's Singapore site states that 1.6 mm is the minimum tread depth, below which the tyre must be replaced. It also says Michelin designs its tyres to keep performing until they reach that limit, so replacing early wastes money. A small raised block in the grooves, the wear indicator, shows when the limit is reached. On Michelin tyres it is marked with a small Michelin Man. See [our guide on when to replace tyres in Singapore](/tyre-guides/when-to-replace-car-tyres-singapore/) for how to check.`},{type:`h3`,text:`Run-flat tyres`,id:`run-flat-tyres`},{type:`p`,text:`A run-flat tyre has reinforced sidewalls that carry the weight of the car after a loss of pressure, so you can keep moving instead of stopping at the roadside. Michelin's run-flat tyres are marked ZP or EMT on the sidewall; other makers use other codes. According to Michelin, you can continue for up to about 80 km at up to about 80 km/h, but the real figure depends on load, temperature and road. Michelin says a run-flat may be repaired just once, by a professional, and never if its sidewall says it cannot be repaired. Whether a run-flat can be repaired is decided by each tyre maker, so ask a professional before assuming it can.`},{type:`h2`,text:`17. The main Michelin tyre families, in plain words`,id:`17-the-main-michelin-tyre-families-in-plain-words`},{type:`p`,text:`Michelin's Singapore site groups its passenger-car tyres into families. Here is how they are described, without the marketing language.`},{type:`table`,head:[`Family`,`Who it is aimed at`,`What Michelin emphasises`],rows:[[`**Primacy** (including e.Primacy)`,`Everyday drivers: commuting and family driving`,`Safe performance across the tyre's life and long wear; e.Primacy is for electric, hybrid and low-consumption cars`],[`**Pilot Sport**`,`Sport and premium cars`,`Steering precision and grip in wet and dry; developed with motorsport experience; Pilot Sport 5 is the tyre Michelin Singapore features on its Pilot page`],[`**Energy**`,`Drivers who want lower fuel use`,`Low rolling-resistance construction with silica in the rubber`],[`**Latitude**`,`Crossovers and premium SUVs`,`Designed for SUV weights and for on- and off-road use`]]},{type:`p`,text:`In January 2022 Michelin unveiled the Pilot Sport 5 and the Primacy 4+ together; the Pilot Sport 5 succeeded the Pilot Sport 4.`},{type:`p`,text:`Some Michelin tyres are made specially for a particular car model and are marked on the sidewall. Michelin says such tyres are approved by the car maker, and if your car came with one, it is usually best to replace it with the same marked type. If you are unsure, send us a photo of your sidewall and we can read the marking for you.`},{type:`h3`,text:`Notes for Singapore drivers`,id:`notes-for-singapore-drivers`},{type:`ul`,items:[`**Winter and all-season tyres:** Michelin's winter families (Alpin) are for snow and ice, which Singapore does not have. Tyres for our wet, hot roads are the summer-type families above.`,`**Tyre age matters as much as brand.** Every tyre has a four-digit date code on its sidewall. See our guide on how to read it.`,`**Check the size first.** The right tyre is the one that matches your car's size and load rating, whatever the brand.`]},{type:`h2`,text:`18. Frequently asked questions`,id:`18-frequently-asked-questions`},{type:`p`,text:`**Is the Michelin Man called Bibendum?** Yes. The name comes from the Latin line "Nunc est Bibendum" in an early composition featuring him.`},{type:`p`,text:`**Is Michelin the company behind the Michelin Guide?** Yes. André Michelin created the first Guide in 1900 to help motorists, and the "star" edition followed in 1926.`},{type:`p`,text:`**What did Michelin invent?** Several things, not all in one go: a detachable bicycle tyre (1891), the first car driven on air-filled tyres (1895), the steel-cased tyre (1937) and the radial tyre (patent filed 1946), plus the rail tyre, the Guide and road signs.`},{type:`p`,text:`**Are all Michelin tyres the same?** No. The families above are designed for different needs. Ask us which one fits your car.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Ask us about Michelin tyres for your car`,id:`ask-us-about-michelin-tyres-for-your-car`},{type:`p`,text:`Tell us your car model, the tyre size on your sidewall, and how you drive. We will tell you which Michelin options exist for your size. Message us on WhatsApp.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Sources`,id:`sources`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Michelin Group, "Michelin heritage": https://www.michelin.com/en/group/heritage`,`Michelin Singapore, tyre families (Primacy, Pilot, Energy, Latitude): https://www.michelin.com.sg/auto/browse-tyres/by-family`,`Michelin Singapore, tyre advice: https://www.michelin.com.sg/auto/advice`]},{type:`p`,text:`*Last reviewed: 7 November 2026. This page is independent and is not published, endorsed or approved by Michelin.*`}]},{slug:`which-michelin-for-your-driving`,kind:`guide`,title:`Which Michelin Tyre for How You Actually Drive? Singapore School Runs, Office Commutes and Trips to KL`,short:`School runs, office commutes and trips to KL: which model fits`,description:`Which Michelin tyre suits a Singapore week of school runs, office commutes, weekend classes and drives to Kuala Lumpur, matched to Michelin's own descriptions.`,lead:`Most guides rank tyres by test results. This one starts from your week.`,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`If you drop the kids at school, drive to the office, pick the kids up after work, run to enrichment classes on Saturday and sometimes make the drive to Kuala Lumpur, you do not need to choose on top speed or on price alone. You need a tyre that stops in the wet, stays quiet, lasts, and is still safe when the car is full of children and luggage on a long expressway. Below, each situation is matched to Michelin's own positioning, with our reasoning shown separately.`},{type:`h2`,text:`The quick answer`,id:`the-quick-answer`},{type:`table`,head:[`Your driving`,`Michelin's family positioning`,`Models to look at first`],rows:[[`Weekday school run + office commute, short stop-start trips, some expressway`,`Everyday comfort, quiet, long life`,`**Primacy 5** (sedans, hatchbacks, MPVs); **Primacy SUV+** (SUVs)`],[`Same, in an electric car`,`Quiet, efficient, EV-suitable`,`**e.Primacy**, or Primacy 5, which Michelin says also suits EVs; **Pilot Sport EV** for high-performance EVs`],[`Regular Singapore–KL trips with a full car`,`Wet braking, even wear, long-distance stability`,`**Primacy 5** / **Primacy SUV+** for family cars; **Pilot Sport 5** if you drive a sporty or premium car and like to drive briskly`],[`Long daily mileage, you want tyres to last`,`Longevity`,`**Energy XM2+** (where your size exists) or Primacy 5`],[`You enjoy spirited weekend drives`,`Sport, grip, precise steering`,`**Pilot Sport 5** (current), Pilot Sport 4 / 4 S`],[`Premium SUV with sporty character`,`SUV performance`,`**Latitude Sport 3**, Pilot Sport 4 SUV`]]},{type:`p`,text:`This is a starting point. Size, load rating and your car's original tyre matter more than any table.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Situation 1: the weekday loop (school, office, school again)`,id:`situation-1-the-weekday-loop-school-office-school-again`},{type:`p`,text:`**What this driving is like.** Many short trips, lots of stopping and starting, mixed town and expressway roads, heavy afternoon rain, and a car that is often carrying children. Tyres get hot, and they get used on wet roads often.`},{type:`p`,text:`**What matters most:** braking and grip in the wet; low noise and a comfortable ride (you are in the car for long stretches); long life, because school-run mileage adds up quickly.`},{type:`p`,text:`**What Michelin says.** On its Singapore site Michelin describes the Primacy 5 as an everyday tyre with a comfortable ride and long life, and says it is ranked first for silence and comfort. It lists a quieter tread pattern and a technology called Silent Rib Gen 3 that reduces vibration. In its "which tyre suits your driving style" guide, Michelin places the Primacy 5 with drivers who want a calm, confident daily drive, wet-weather grip and shorter braking distances.`},{type:`p`,text:`**Our view.** For a family car doing the weekday loop, the Primacy family is the one that fits Michelin's own description of this kind of driving. If your car is an SUV, look at the Primacy SUV+. Michelin describes it as a summer SUV tyre focused on wet braking and cites tests by the independent lab CATARC on a Honda CR-V (225/65 R17 106H) against premium competitors: new-tyre wet braking distance shorter by 3.78 m (13.3%), and shorter by 5.26 m (12%) when worn to 2 mm of tread. Michelin notes that its wear-life test was done at Michelin's request, and results will differ by size and car.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Situation 2: Saturday classes, errands and the mall`,id:`situation-2-saturday-classes-errands-and-the-mall`},{type:`p`,text:`This is the weekday pattern with more passengers and more luggage: swim bags, scooters, groceries. The tyre advice is the same, with one addition: **tyre pressure**.`},{type:`p`,text:`Michelin says carmakers give two pressures, a normal one and a **loaded** one for when the car is heavily laden. The figures are on the sticker in the driver's door frame or in the manual. Michelin also warns that being 1 bar (14 psi) under the recommended pressure can add about 11 metres to wet braking distance, and that tyres lose about 0.07 bar a month naturally. If your car was built after 2014 it should have a pressure monitor, but Michelin recommends not waiting for the warning light. A five-minute pressure check at a petrol station once a month is one of the simplest safety habits there is.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Situation 3: Singapore to Kuala Lumpur`,id:`situation-3-singapore-to-kuala-lumpur`},{type:`p`,text:`**What this driving is like.** Long hours at steady speed on expressways, a fully loaded car (family plus luggage), heavy rain at short notice, and usually a fixed time window, so nobody wants a breakdown.`},{type:`p`,text:`**Before you go,** Michelin's road-trip checklist says to check:`},{type:`ol`,items:[`**Wear.** Make sure wear is even and not too far gone before a long journey. If a tyre is close to its limit, replace it before the trip, not after.`,`**Pressure on all four tyres and the spare,** remembering that the loaded pressure may differ.`,`**The spare, jack and wheel spanner.**`,`**Luggage.** Spread it across the car and keep items off the rear parcel shelf, where they can block the mirror view and become dangerous in a crash.`,`**Seat belts and child seats.** Michelin points out that rules differ from country to country, so check the requirements of every country you drive in.`,`**Rest.** Michelin advises getting enough sleep first, so a rested early start beats leaving late after a full day at work.`]},{type:`p`,text:`**Tyre choice for this driving.** The things that matter most are wet braking, even wear, comfort over long distances and a tyre that is not near the end of its life. That is the same list as the weekday loop, so the Primacy family is again the natural fit for a family sedan, MPV or SUV. If you drive a sporty or premium car and like a brisk drive, Michelin positions the Pilot Sport 5 for drivers who want grip and precise handling on wet motorways and long-distance road trips; according to Michelin it typically carries a Y speed rating, though that depends on the size. Michelin describes it as a sport tyre designed for high longevity.`},{type:`p`,text:`**Our view.** For a car that is mostly a family car, we would not pick a sport tyre just because the trip is long. Choose the tyre for the 95% of your week that is school runs and commuting, and make sure it is in good condition before the KL drive.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Situation 4: electric cars`,id:`situation-4-electric-cars`},{type:`p`,text:`Michelin says the Primacy 5 is also suitable for EVs, because its tread pattern is designed to reduce the noise that is more noticeable in a quiet electric car. For EVs Michelin also lists the e.Primacy, a summer tyre it says is chosen by carmakers for electric, hybrid and lower-fuel-use cars, and the Pilot Sport EV, a high-performance summer tyre for electric vehicles. Rolling resistance matters more in an EV because it affects range; see [our Michelin guide](/tyre-brands/michelin/) for how Michelin explains it.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Situation 5: you drive a lot and want tyres to last`,id:`situation-5-you-drive-a-lot-and-want-tyres-to-last`},{type:`p`,text:`If your mileage is high and your size is available, look at the Energy XM2+. Michelin describes it as a long-lasting summer tyre built for city commutes and weekend drives and says it gives about 25% more mileage than the average of premium competitor tyres. As with any manufacturer comparison, treat the number as Michelin's claim, not a promise for your car.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Situation 6: you like driving, and the weekend is yours`,id:`situation-6-you-like-driving-and-the-weekend-is-yours`},{type:`p`,text:`For drivers who like responsive steering and grip, Michelin positions the **Pilot Sport 5** (fifth generation, sport summer tyre, with MaxTouch Construction for even force distribution and Dynamic Response Technology for steering response) and the earlier Pilot Sport 4 and 4 S. For SUVs, Michelin describes the **Latitude Sport 3** as a premium SUV tyre built for dynamic handling and wet braking, and the **Pilot Sport 4 SUV** as a premium, comfortable high-performance SUV tyre. Pilot Sport Cup 2 is aimed at track days and is not a family-car tyre.`},{type:`h2`,text:`How to check what you have now`,id:`how-to-check-what-you-have-now`},{type:`p`,text:`Look at the sidewall: the tyre name, then a size code like 225/45 R17 94W. Also look for the four-digit date code (week and year of manufacture); see our guide on reading it. Photograph it and send it to us.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Common questions`,id:`common-questions`},{type:`p`,text:`**Do I need a different tyre for Malaysia?** Michelin's road-trip advice says to make sure your tyres suit the weather and conditions of your route. For Singapore and Malaysia that means a summer-type tyre with good wet performance. Michelin's winter families are not relevant.`},{type:`p`,text:`**Is a more expensive or sportier tyre safer for a long trip?** Not automatically. A tyre in good condition, at the right pressure, and suited to your car and load, matters more than category. We do not discuss prices on this page; message us for options in your size.`},{type:`p`,text:`**How do I know a tyre is too worn?** Michelin states that 1.6 mm is the minimum tread depth, and wear indicators in the grooves show when you have reached it. Do not wait until the limit before a long trip.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Ask us`,id:`ask-us`},{type:`p`,text:`Tell us your car model, the size on your sidewall, and how you drive (school run, office, KL trips, spirited weekends). We will tell you which Michelin options exist in your size. Message us on WhatsApp.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Sources`,id:`sources`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Michelin Singapore, "Which Michelin tyre suits your driving style?": https://www.michelin.com.sg/auto/advice/choose-tyres/michelin-tyre-based-on-your-driving-profile`,`Michelin Singapore, Primacy 5, Primacy SUV+, Energy XM2+, Pilot Sport 5 product pages: https://www.michelin.com.sg/auto/tyres/michelin-primacy-5 and following`,`Michelin Singapore, "Get ready for a road trip (checklist)": https://www.michelin.com.sg/auto/advice/tyre-care/road-trip-checklist`,`Michelin Singapore, "What tyre pressure?": https://www.michelin.com.sg/auto/advice/tyre-pressure/what-tyre-pressure`,`Michelin Singapore, tread depth and rolling resistance advice pages`]},{type:`p`,text:`*Last reviewed: 7 November 2026. Independent page, not published, endorsed or approved by Michelin.*`}]},{slug:`primacy-5`,kind:`model`,title:`Michelin Primacy 5 Review: Who Should Buy It, and When to Pick Something Else`,short:`Comfort, quiet and mileage: who should buy it and who should not`,description:`A plain review of the Michelin Primacy 5: what Michelin claims, what independent tests found, how it compares with the Energy XM2+, and who it suits in Singapore.`,lead:`**Verdict in one minute.** The Primacy 5 is the Michelin to look at if your week is made of school runs, office commutes and the occasional drive up to Malaysia. Michelin sells it on comfort, quietness, mileage and braking in the wet. Independent tests back the comfort, wear and efficiency story, but they do not show it winning on grip, so it is a "sensible and refined" tyre, not a "fastest around the corner" one. If you want sharp steering, look at the Pilot Sport 5. If your car is an electric one and range is your first worry, read [our guide to Michelin's EV tyres](/tyre-brands/michelin/ev-tyres/).`,blocks:[{type:`h2`,text:`What the Primacy 5 is for`,id:`what-the-primacy-5-is-for`},{type:`p`,text:`Michelin's own wording is "a confident choice for everyday driving with a premium comfort ride and excellent longevity". In its "which tyre suits your driving style" guide it gives this tyre to drivers who treat the car as a calm place to be: quiet cabin, reliable braking in the rain, and a tyre that is not worn out by the next service.`},{type:`p`,text:`That is a useful clue about who should buy it. If you recognise yourself in the description, you will probably be happy. If you read it and think "that sounds dull", you are a Pilot Sport driver and you should skip this page.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Three claims, and what stands behind each`,id:`three-claims-and-what-stands-behind-each`},{type:`h3`,text:`1. "Quiet and comfortable"`,id:`1-quiet-and-comfortable`},{type:`p`,text:`Michelin says the Primacy 5 is ranked first for silence and comfort and credits a new tread pattern and a vibration-damping technology it calls Silent Rib Gen 3. The evidence Michelin shows is from tests it ordered: TÜV Rheinland Thailand measured a Honda Accord e:HEV fitted with 225/50 R17 tyres in November 2024, and later a large SUV on 265/60 R18 in November 2025, where Michelin reports 3% (1.64 dB) lower interior noise than other premium brands. A difference of 1.64 dB is small. Real-world quietness also depends on your road surface and your car's insulation, so treat "quiet" as "among the quieter tyres", not as a promise of silence.`},{type:`h3`,text:`2. "Safe in the wet, new or worn"`,id:`2-safe-in-the-wet-new-or-worn`},{type:`p`,text:`This is the claim to weigh most carefully, because it matters in Singapore's downpours. Michelin reports shorter braking from 80 to 0 km/h than the average of other premium brands: 8% shorter new and 13% shorter worn on the Accord, 16% shorter in both states on the SUV. Those are Michelin-ordered tests. Other comparisons are less one-sided. In a 2024 test of five premium touring tyres in 205/55 R16 on a Golf, run by the labs TÜV SÜD and DEKRA and reported by Tyre Reviews, the Continental PremiumContact 7 had the shortest wet and dry braking, while the Primacy 5 was best on wear and efficiency. That test was commissioned by Michelin, so it is not an independent cross-check of Michelin's own figures, but it does show where a rival came out ahead. A fair reading is that its wet behaviour is good, that it is not the braking leader in every test, and that its older sibling, the Primacy 4, was the one Michelin designed around wet braking above all else.`},{type:`h3`,text:`3. "Lasts longer"`,id:`3-lasts-longer`},{type:`p`,text:`Michelin says the Primacy 5 gives 18% more mileage than the Primacy 4+, measured on an electric vehicle, and that DEKRA measured 24% longer life than other premium brands on a Golf VIII with 205/55 R16 tyres. The same Michelin-commissioned test agrees that wear and efficiency are its strengths, and Tyre Reviews' own weighting of it (not a result issued by the labs) put the Primacy 5 and the PremiumContact 7 in joint first place, the Michelin ahead on abrasion and rolling resistance and the Continental ahead on braking. Tyre Reviews' statistics page also shows an average finish of about fourth across the tests it tracks, with a best of first and a worst of seventh. Mileage depends heavily on your driving, your alignment and your tyre pressure, so use these numbers to compare tyres with each other, not to predict your own kilometres.`},{type:`h2`,text:`How it fits among Michelin's everyday tyres`,id:`how-it-fits-among-michelin-s-everyday-tyres`},{type:`table`,head:[`If you care most about`,`Look at`],rows:[[`Comfort, quiet and a balanced all-rounder`,`Primacy 5`],[`Lowest rolling resistance for an electric or hybrid car`,`e.Primacy (see [our EV guide](/tyre-brands/michelin/ev-tyres/))`],[`Maximum mileage on a compact or mid-size car`,`Energy XM2+, below`],[`Sporty steering and dry grip`,`Pilot Sport 5`]]},{type:`h3`,text:`A note on the Energy XM2+`,id:`energy-xm2-plus`},{type:`p`,text:`The Energy XM2+ is an older long-life design that Michelin sells for "city commutes and weekend drives". Michelin says it gives about 25% more mileage than the average of premium competitors; that figure comes from a CATARC test Michelin ordered in September 2018, run over 20,000 km on a VW Golf 7 with 205/55 R16 tyres. We found no fully independent long-term wear test. Reports of its launch date point to January 2020 as the successor to the Energy XM2. It does not claim the Primacy 5's quietness, so if you drive a lot and price per kilometre matters more than refinement, ask us whether your size exists in the XM2+ as well as the Primacy 5.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Where the Primacy 5 came from`,id:`where-the-primacy-5-came-from`},{type:`p`,text:`Michelin unveiled the Primacy 4+ in January 2022, alongside the Pilot Sport 5, and the Primacy 5 is its successor. We could not find an official launch date for the Primacy 5 itself. Michelin describes the shift between generations like this: the Primacy 4 emphasised wet braking, the Primacy 5 emphasises comfort and efficiency while still aiming at strong wet braking from the first kilometre to the last.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Before you buy`,id:`before-you-buy`},{type:`ol`,items:[`Photograph your current tyre's sidewall: the size, load index and speed rating must match or exceed what your car needs.`,`Read the four-digit date code on any tyre you are offered, so you know how old it is.`,`If you tow, carry heavy loads or have a long KL drive planned, tell us; load rating and pressure matter more than the brand name.`]},{type:`h2`,text:`Questions people ask about the Primacy 5`,id:`questions-people-ask-about-the-primacy-5`},{type:`p`,text:`**Is it really quiet on a Singapore expressway?** Michelin's tests show a small noise advantage; road surface matters as much. If noise is your main worry, ask about your specific car.`},{type:`p`,text:`**Does it suit an electric car?** Michelin says yes, and it highlights the noise-reducing tread as more noticeable in an EV. For maximum range, the e.Primacy is Michelin's dedicated option.`},{type:`p`,text:`**Is it good in heavy rain?** It is a sound wet tyre, and Michelin's figures are strong, but independent results do not put it at the very top. Keep the pressure right and replace it before it is worn out.`},{type:`p`,text:`**Primacy 5 or Pilot Sport 5?** Comfort and mileage against steering feel and grip. Choose the one that matches how you drive on a normal Tuesday.`},{type:`p`,text:`*Sources: Michelin Singapore product page for the Primacy 5 and Energy XM2+; Michelin's "which tyre suits your driving style" guide; Tyre Reviews' test listings for the Primacy 5; Rushlane (January 2020) on the Energy XM2+ launch; Paultan (31 January 2022) on the Primacy 4+ and Pilot Sport 5. Michelin-ordered tests are labelled as such. Last reviewed 7 November 2026.*`}]},{slug:`pilot-sport-5`,kind:`model`,title:`Michelin Pilot Sport 5: Six Questions Drivers Ask Before Buying`,short:`Michelin's current sport road tyre in six questions`,description:`The Michelin Pilot Sport 5 explained through six questions: who it is for, what changed from the Pilot Sport 4, wet performance, and how it differs from the 4 S.`,lead:`The Pilot Sport 5 is Michelin's current sport tyre for the road. Michelin introduced it in January 2022 together with the Primacy 4+, and called it the successor to the Pilot Sport 4. Below are the six questions we hear most often, answered with what Michelin says and what other people's tests found.`,blocks:[{type:`h2`,text:`1. Is it a tyre for my car?`,id:`1-is-it-a-tyre-for-my-car`},{type:`p`,text:`Michelin's answer is "drivers who want precise steering and grip, and a tyre that lasts longer than older sport tyres". In its driving-style guide it hands the Pilot Sport 5 to the driver who is "always ready, usually packed", someone who takes spontaneous road trips and wants confidence on wet motorways.`},{type:`p`,text:`In practice that means a sporty or premium car, or any car whose owner enjoys driving. A family MPV used for school runs does not need it; the Primacy 5 is the tyre Michelin recommends for that life. Press reports at launch said it came in about 50 sizes from 17 to 21 inches, so it is also aimed at cars with larger wheels.`},{type:`h2`,text:`2. What actually changed from the Pilot Sport 4?`,id:`2-what-actually-changed-from-the-pilot-sport-4`},{type:`p`,text:`Michelin lists these, in its own words, as the main differences: better grip on wet roads, a tougher tread that lasts longer, a more refined feel, a premium-looking sidewall finish and a wider range of sizes. Three named technologies carry the story:`},{type:`ul`,items:[`**MaxTouch Construction.** Michelin says it spreads the forces of accelerating, braking and cornering more evenly across the part of the tyre that touches the road, which should slow wear.`,`**Dynamic Response Technology.** The tyre structure is designed so that steering input reaches the road quickly, which is where the "precise" feeling comes from.`,`**Dual Sport Tread Design.** One half of the tread has wide grooves for pushing water away, the other has stiffer blocks for dry grip.`]},{type:`p`,text:`Michelin also states a typical speed rating of Y, which means over 300 km/h under controlled conditions. You will not use that on a Singapore road, but it tells you the tyre is built to a high standard of heat and stress tolerance.`},{type:`h2`,text:`3. Is it good in the rain?`,id:`3-is-it-good-in-the-rain`},{type:`p`,text:`Michelin describes the Pilot Sport 5 as a "100% road tyre" whose first priority is wet performance, so that the driver can push hard "in full control". We looked for independent test placings to set against that. The summaries we could read were inconsistent about which tyres won the 2022 and 2023 tests that included the Pilot Sport 5, and we could not open the original reports, so we are not publishing placings we cannot confirm. What we can say is that aggregator listings generally show it among the better wet performers rather than at the bottom, and that its predecessor, the Pilot Sport 4, has a well-documented record of test wins, which we set out on our Pilot Sport 4 and 4 S page. If wet braking is your deciding factor, ask us for the latest independent tests in your size and we will tell you what they show.`},{type:`h2`,text:`4. How is it different from the Pilot Sport 4 S?`,id:`4-how-is-it-different-from-the-pilot-sport-4-s`},{type:`p`,text:`Michelin says the two are "complementary". The Pilot Sport 5 is a pure road tyre, designed for wet performance, steering precision on dry roads and longevity. The Pilot Sport 4 S is aimed at the most powerful cars and, in Michelin's words, is about 80% road and 20% track, built for maximum dry grip. If you do not drive on a circuit, Michelin's own comparison points you to the 5. We cover the 4 and 4 S on their own page.`},{type:`h2`,text:`5. Is it quiet and comfortable?`,id:`5-is-it-quiet-and-comfortable`},{type:`p`,text:`Michelin does not make a silence claim for it on its Singapore page. Sport tyres generally trade some comfort for steering feel; in an older test the Pilot Sport 4 S, a sibling, was marked down for noise. If a quiet cabin is your priority, choose the Primacy 5.`},{type:`h2`,text:`6. Will it fit, and how do I know?`,id:`6-will-it-fit-and-how-do-i-know`},{type:`p`,text:`Not every size exists, and some manufacturers approve particular tyres. Read the size on your sidewall (for example 225/45 R17 94W), check the load and speed ratings against your car's requirements, and send us the photo. We will tell you whether the Pilot Sport 5 exists in your size and what the alternatives are.`},{type:`p`,text:`*Sources: Michelin Singapore Pilot Sport 5 page and driving-style guide; Paultan (31 January 2022); Tyre Reviews test listings for the Pilot Sport 5 (placings not quoted, see section 3). Last reviewed 7 November 2026.*`}]},{slug:`pilot-sport-4-and-4s`,kind:`model`,title:`Michelin Pilot Sport 4 and Pilot Sport 4 S: Two Sport Tyres, Two Different Jobs`,short:`Two older sport tyres and the tests they won`,description:`Michelin Pilot Sport 4 and Pilot Sport 4 S compared: what each is for, a timeline of independent test results, and which driver should still choose them.`,lead:`Before the Pilot Sport 5, Michelin's performance road range was built on two tyres that look alike in a size chart and are meant for different people. This page explains the difference and what tests have found, so you can judge whether an older design is still worth buying.`,blocks:[{type:`h2`,text:`The short answer`,id:`the-short-answer`},{type:`ul`,items:[`**Pilot Sport 4:** the all-round sport tyre. Michelin describes it as a high-performance summer road tyre with high reactivity on wet roads and "safety made to last". It won several magazine tests, as set out below.`,`**Pilot Sport 4 S:** the maximum-grip version. Michelin says it is for road and occasional track use, for drivers who "expect exceptional grip, steering precision and control, even at high speed", and notes that carmakers such as Mercedes-AMG, Ferrari and Porsche have chosen it.`]},{type:`p`,text:`If your car is a hot hatch or a quick saloon, the 4 is usually the better fit. If it is genuinely powerful, or if it sometimes goes to a track, the 4 S is the one designed for that.`},{type:`h2`,text:`How the two measured up in independent tests`,id:`how-the-two-measured-up-in-independent-tests`},{type:`p`,text:`These results come from Tyre Reviews and other aggregator sites that summarise Auto Bild's tests; we could not open the original German reports. They are several years old, so newer tyres may have caught up.`},{type:`table`,head:[`Year and test`,`Tyre`,`What was reported`],rows:[[`Auto Bild UHP test, 2017 (245/40 R18, nine tyres)`,`Pilot Sport 4`,`Overall winner`],[`Auto Bild, 2018 (245/30 R20, eight tyres, Mercedes E-Class)`,`Pilot Sport 4 S`,`First place with 32 points, but reported as the loudest tyre in the group`],[`Auto Bild summer test, 2019 (225/45 R17, 20 tyres)`,`Pilot Sport 4`,`First of 20; Auto Bild praised its rolling resistance and mileage but noted a modest aquaplaning safety margin. One other site lists a different winner for this size, so treat the placing with care`],[`Auto Bild UHP 19-inch, 2020 (265/35 R19, ten tyres)`,`Pilot Sport 4 S`,`Joint winner with the Continental SportContact 6, and the more expensive of the two`]]},{type:`h2`,text:`What the pattern tells you`,id:`what-the-pattern-tells-you`},{type:`ol`,items:[`**Wet performance is the common thread.** Both tyres won tests that weighted wet braking and handling, which matters in Singapore.`,`**The 4 S pays for its grip with noise and cost.** In the tests above it was the loudest in one and the pricier of two winners in another.`,`**The tests are old.** The Pilot Sport 5 arrived in 2022, and Michelin says it improves wet grip and tread life over the 4. If your size is available in the 5, compare it before choosing an older tyre; if it is not, the 4 is still a proven design.`]},{type:`h2`,text:`Which one for which driver`,id:`which-one-for-which-driver`},{type:`table`,head:[`Your situation`,`Better fit`],rows:[[`Quick saloon or hot hatch, road driving only`,`Pilot Sport 4 (or 5 if available in your size)`],[`High-powered car with occasional track days`,`Pilot Sport 4 S`],[`You want the quietest, most comfortable tyre`,`Neither: choose the Primacy 5`],[`SUV`,`Pilot Sport 4 SUV, covered on [our SUV page](/tyre-brands/michelin/suv-tyres/)`]]},{type:`h2`,text:`Before you decide`,id:`before-you-decide`},{type:`p`,text:`Check the date code on any tyre you are offered. Older designs sit in warehouses, and a tyre's age matters as much as its model. Send us your sidewall photo and we will tell you which of these exist in your size and how old the stock is.`},{type:`p`,text:`*Sources: Michelin Singapore pages for the Pilot Sport 4 and 4 S; Michelin's Pilot Sport 5 comparison with the 4 S; Tyre Reviews listings for the 2017 and 2020 Auto Bild tests, the 2019 Auto Bild summer test and the Pilot Sport 4 test results. Last reviewed 7 November 2026.*`}]},{slug:`suv-tyres`,kind:`model`,title:`Choosing a Michelin Tyre for Your SUV: Pilot Sport 4 SUV, Primacy SUV+ or Latitude Sport 3`,short:`Pilot Sport 4 SUV, Primacy SUV+ and Latitude Sport 3 compared`,description:`Choosing a Michelin tyre for an SUV in Singapore: Pilot Sport 4 SUV, Primacy SUV+ and Latitude Sport 3 compared, with Michelin's claims and the independent test evidence.`,lead:`Three Michelin summer tyres are sold for SUVs and crossovers in Singapore, and they answer three different questions. This page helps you pick by asking those questions in order, then gives the evidence for each tyre.`,blocks:[{type:`h2`,text:`Step 1: how heavy is your daily load?`,id:`step-1-how-heavy-is-your-daily-load`},{type:`p`,text:`An SUV is heavier than a sedan, and it is often fuller: children, bags, a stroller, sometimes a long drive. Heavier loads lengthen braking distances and heat the tyres more. Before you choose a brand, check two numbers on your sidewall: the load index (the number after the size) and the speed rating (the letter). They must meet or exceed what your carmaker specifies. Michelin also tells drivers to use the loaded pressure printed on the car's door sticker when the car is fully laden, and warns that being 1 bar under pressure can add about 11 metres to wet braking.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Step 2: what do you want from the tyre?`,id:`step-2-what-do-you-want-from-the-tyre`},{type:`table`,head:[`If your priority is`,`Michelin tyre to look at first`],rows:[[`Wet braking and mileage in everyday use`,`Primacy SUV+`],[`Sporty feel and strong wet braking on a family SUV`,`Pilot Sport 4 SUV`],[`A premium SUV, particularly one fitted with it from new`,`Latitude Sport 3`]]},{type:`h2`,text:`Primacy SUV+: wet braking that Michelin says lasts`,id:`primacy-suv-plus`},{type:`p`,text:`**What Michelin says.** The Primacy SUV+ is a summer SUV tyre "for safety across your SUV driving journey" with excellent braking in the rain. Michelin credits a new generation of EverGrip tread technology: a rubber compound built into the pattern to clear water, with U-shaped grooves and full-depth slits meant to keep working as the tread wears. For 18-inch and larger sizes it has a velvet-textured sidewall finish.`},{type:`p`,text:`**The numbers.** Michelin cites tests by CATARC on a Honda CR-V with 225/65 R17 106H tyres, against premium rivals: braking from 80 km/h to 20 km/h in the wet was 3.78 m (13.3%) shorter when new and 5.26 m (12%) shorter when the tread had worn to 2 mm, and braking from 100 km/h to a standstill on dry roads was 3.67 m (9.0%) shorter. A wear test run at Michelin's request estimated a 10% longer life. All of these are Michelin-ordered figures and Michelin itself notes results differ in other conditions.`},{type:`p`,text:`**What we could not find.** An independent magazine test of the Primacy SUV+, and an official launch date. We will not borrow results from other tyres.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Pilot Sport 4 SUV: the one with the test record`,id:`pilot-sport-4-suv`},{type:`p`,text:`**What Michelin says.** A premium, comfortable high-performance SUV tyre with excellent grip on dry roads and braking on wet roads, a robust tread for longevity, and a design that aims to cut noise. It uses the same Dynamic Response and Dual Sport Tread ideas as the Pilot Sport car tyres.`},{type:`p`,text:`**What the tests found.** Summaries on Tyre Reviews report:`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Auto Bild Allrad, 2020 (255/55 R18, ten tyres): first place, with an advantage of nearly a metre in dry braking over the nearest rival; Tyre Reviews notes the high purchase price as its main drawback.`,`Auto Bild Allrad, 2023 (225/65 R17, twelve tyres): overall test winner, with the shortest wet braking (49.90 m from 100 km/h, against 52.10 m for the Goodyear EfficientGrip 2 SUV); a Pirelli beat it in dry braking. Tyre Reviews notes this is a performance tyre tested largely against touring-style rivals.`,`We did not use later tests because we could not confirm them.`]},{type:`p`,text:`**The trade-off.** Wet braking is its proven strength; quietness is not. If your SUV is a family car used mostly for commuting and school runs, compare it against the Primacy SUV+.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Latitude Sport 3: older, premium-focused`,id:`latitude-sport-3`},{type:`p`,text:`**What Michelin says.** A high-performance SUV summer tyre "acclaimed by premium car manufacturers", with a compound and central rib designed for precise handling and wet braking.`},{type:`p`,text:`**What the tests found.** We found no recent independent test that we could verify, and we are not repeating older figures we could not confirm. A tyre-comparison site dates its European launch to early 2014. It is a design to choose mainly when your car was fitted with it, or when your size is not available in the other two.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Which should you pick?`,id:`which-should-you-pick`},{type:`ol`,items:[`If your SUV carries children and bags every day and you want the lowest-stress wet driving, start with the Primacy SUV+, accepting that the evidence is Michelin's own.`,`If you want sportier handling and an independent track record, the Pilot Sport 4 SUV is the better documented.`,`If your car came with the Latitude Sport 3 or has a marking from the carmaker, tell us; replacing like with like is usually safest.`]},{type:`p`,text:`Send us your sidewall photo and your car model, and we will tell you which of the three exist in your size.`},{type:`p`,text:`*Sources: Michelin Singapore pages for the Primacy SUV+, Pilot Sport 4 SUV and Latitude Sport 3, and its tyre-pressure advice; Tyre Reviews' Pilot Sport 4 SUV test results and 2023 SUV test; Whattyre listing for the Latitude Sport 3 (launch date only). Last reviewed 7 November 2026.*`}]},{slug:`ev-tyres`,kind:`model`,title:`Michelin Tyres for Electric Cars: e.Primacy, Pilot Sport EV and Primacy 5 Compared`,short:`e.Primacy, Pilot Sport EV and Primacy 5 for electric cars`,description:`Why electric cars are hard on tyres, and how Michelin's e.Primacy, Pilot Sport EV and Primacy 5 compare, including rolling resistance and aquaplaning test results.`,lead:`Electric cars are hard on tyres, and Singapore has plenty of them. This page explains why, and then looks at the three Michelin tyres you are most likely to be offered for an EV, including the trade-offs that the brochures leave out.`,blocks:[{type:`h2`,text:`Why an EV is not just a car with a battery`,id:`why-an-ev-is-not-just-a-car-with-a-battery`},{type:`p`,text:`Three things set EVs apart for tyres:`},{type:`ol`,items:[`**Weight.** A battery pack adds a lot of mass, which puts more load on every tyre and can wear them faster.`,`**Instant torque.** An electric motor delivers its pull the moment you press the pedal, which can scrub the tread, especially on wet roads.`,`**Silence.** With no engine noise to mask it, road noise from the tyres is more noticeable.`]},{type:`p`,text:`On top of those, many owners care about **range**, and rolling resistance is one of the few tyre properties that moves it. Michelin's Singapore advice page explains rolling resistance as the energy lost as the tyre deforms, spins through the air and rubs on the road, and adds that tyre pressure has a direct effect: it cites French road studies where under-inflation of 0.3 bar added about 6% to rolling resistance.`},{type:`h2`,text:`e.Primacy: lowest rolling resistance, with one catch`,id:`e-primacy`},{type:`p`,text:`**What Michelin says.** The e.Primacy is "for electric, hybrid or low fuel consumption vehicles" and is chosen by carmakers as original equipment. Michelin claims rolling resistance 27% lower on average than leading competitors, credits an energy-passive compound and a slim belt that uses less material, and says electric-car drivers can expect extended range. A test site also reports a Michelin claim of up to about 7% extra range, which we could not verify independently.`},{type:`p`,text:`**What the tests found.** Summaries on Tyre Reviews and similar sites report:`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Rolling resistance: first in a 2025 AMS sustainability test (5.7 kg/t), and best in test by a clear margin at Teknikens Värld in 2025, where it placed second of eight overall in 235/55 R19.`,`Water: Tyre Reviews' summary lists curved and straight aquaplaning among its weak points, though the summaries do not say which test that comes from. We could not confirm the ranking.`]},{type:`p`,text:`**The catch.** Very low rolling resistance is well supported, while aquaplaning resistance is listed as a weak point in the summaries we found. In Singapore's sudden storms, that matters. Keep the pressure right, slow down in standing water and replace the tyres before the tread is low.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Pilot Sport EV: performance for powerful electric cars`,id:`pilot-sport-ev`},{type:`p`,text:`**What Michelin says.** A high-performance summer tyre for electric vehicles, "inspired by Formula E", with 15% more cornering stiffness, a compound with a stiff centre profile to carry torque, long-lasting wet grip and reduced noise. Michelin has also published an internal comparison showing 6.7 kg/t rolling resistance against 8.8 kg/t for the Pilot Sport 4 SUV on 255/45 R19 tyres in October 2020; we could not verify it.`},{type:`p`,text:`**What the tests found.** In Auto Bild's 2024 test of tyres for EVs (235/55 R19), the Pilot Sport EV was run alongside the other contenders. Tyre Reviews lists it ninth, while another report says it was left out of the final results table, so we cannot give you a reliable placing. The test was won by Michelin's own Pilot Sport 4 SUV. We could not retrieve the results of a 2023 test of EV performance tyres. The independent evidence does not yet match the marketing, so compare it with alternatives for your specific car.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Primacy 5: the comfort-first option Michelin also recommends for EVs`,id:`primacy-5-for-evs`},{type:`p`,text:`Michelin says the Primacy 5 is "fully suitable for EV", pointing to its tread pattern, which it designed to reduce the noise that is more noticeable in an EV, and to lower rolling resistance than its predecessor. It is not an EV-specific tyre, so ask us how it compares with the e.Primacy for your particular car. See [our Primacy 5 review](/tyre-brands/michelin/primacy-5/) for the details.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Choosing among them`,id:`choosing-among-them`},{type:`table`,head:[`Your priority`,`Start with`],rows:[[`Maximum range, mostly dry-weather commuting`,`e.Primacy (mind the aquaplaning result)`],[`Quiet, comfortable, balanced`,`Primacy 5`],[`A powerful EV, sporty driving`,`Pilot Sport EV, but compare it with alternatives first`]]},{type:`h2`,text:`Practical points for EV owners`,id:`practical-points-for-ev-owners`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Check the load index: a heavy EV needs tyres rated for its weight.`,`Rotate regularly: heavier cars with strong torque can wear the driven axle's tyres faster.`,`Check pressure monthly with the car loaded to its normal state.`]},{type:`p`,text:`Send us your car model and sidewall photo and we will tell you which of these exist in your size.`},{type:`p`,text:`*Sources: Michelin Singapore pages for the e.Primacy, Pilot Sport EV and Primacy 5; Michelin's rolling-resistance advice page; Tyre Reviews and aggregator summaries for the test results named. Last reviewed 7 November 2026.*`}]}]}]};function t(){return e.brands}function n(e){return t().find(t=>t.slug===e)}function r(e,t){return n(e)?.pages.find(e=>e.slug===t)}function i(e){let t=[],n=/\*\*([^*]+)\*\*|\*([^*]+)\*|\[([^\]]+)\]\(([^)\s]+)\)/g,r=0;for(let i of e.matchAll(n)){let n=i.index??0;n>r&&t.push({t:`text`,v:e.slice(r,n)}),i[1]===void 0?i[2]===void 0?/^(\/|https:\/\/)/.test(i[4]??``)?t.push({t:`a`,v:i[3]??``,href:i[4]??``}):t.push({t:`text`,v:i[0]}):t.push({t:`i`,v:i[2]}):t.push({t:`b`,v:i[1]}),r=n+i[0].length}return r<e.length&&t.push({t:`text`,v:e.slice(r)}),t}export{i,r as n,t as r,n as t};